WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.435 --> 00:00:05.985
[Narrator] Season three of Netflix's

00:00:05.985 --> 00:00:09.084
hit '80s sci-fi series, "Stranger Things,"

00:00:09.084 --> 00:00:12.030
culminates in a main-character death.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:12.863
Or does it?

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.810
We're going to take a look at the ending

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.680
and all-important post-credits scene.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.546
Spoilers are coming if you
haven't seen the season yet.

00:00:22.723 --> 00:00:25.270
Season three ends with
Hopper (David Harbour)

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.470
and Joyce (Winona Ryder)
attempting to close the gate

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.410
between this world and the Upside Down.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.250
Hopper is standing next to the machine

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.991
that controls the gate,

00:00:34.991 --> 00:00:36.940
and he disappears before the blast.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.760
Later scenes show the
other characters mourning,

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.853
and Eleven moves out of
Hawkins with the Byers family.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.736
So here's why Hopper is
probably still alive.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.000
Instead of rolling to
the usual end credits,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.220
the camera pans over the
skyline of Kamchatka, Russia.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.730
This snowy outcrop houses a
mysterious Soviet facility.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:03.850
A soldier passes a doorway
to unlock a prisoner,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:08.080
and another soldier says,
"No, not the American."

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.830
They then unlock a second door,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:13.230
dragging a man out and
feeding him to a demogorgon.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.848
It's unclear if this is the
same demogorgon from season one.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.675
There are very few candidates

00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:23.220
for who "the American" could be.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.360
Either it's a whole new character

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.670
or someone presumed dead
in previous seasons.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.541
Dr. Brenner from season one

00:01:30.541 --> 00:01:32.391
was a research scientist in Hawkins

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:34.450
who was killed by a demogorgon.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.330
But in season two, a former lab employee

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.480
tells Eleven that Brenner survived.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.455
Aside from him, there isn't
anyone else whose death

00:01:43.455 --> 00:01:46.610
has remained an ambiguity
to such an extent.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.920
It seems too much of a coincidence

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.193
that the Soviets call Hopper
"the American" all season.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.430
Of course, for this to be Hopper,

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:56.932
he would've had to survive the blast

00:01:56.932 --> 00:01:59.250
in the Starcourt Mall basement.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.030
We know that he wasn't able to get back

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.860
through the glass divide to Joyce

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.370
because of the electrical charge

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:07.650
caused by the machine
after Grigori's death.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.400
In the opening of the season,

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.920
we see exactly what happens
to anyone in the blast radius.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.200
Their bodies are overtaken
by blue tendrils of energy,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.300
apparently electrocuted.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.600
This also occurs immediately
after the machine is detonated.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.920
This is different with Hopper.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.850
We don't actually see any
physical effects from the blast,

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.950
because he just vanishes.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.573
Here is where he stood
when Joyce turns the keys.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.840
Look again one second
after she turns them.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.763
He's not standing there anymore.

00:02:40.197 --> 00:02:43.242
Most important, the blast in episode one

00:02:43.242 --> 00:02:45.110
leaves behind bodies.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.085
We see the technicians getting fried

00:02:47.085 --> 00:02:49.109
and then spot their corpses,

00:02:49.109 --> 00:02:51.710
whereas there isn't even a
partially melted mustache

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:52.601
for Hopper.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.778
Hopper is also standing
extremely close to the gate,

00:02:56.778 --> 00:02:59.380
within jumping distance
of the Upside Down.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:02.290
If he is still alive, he
could've climbed down the ladder

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.240
and jumped into the
crack before it closed.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.580
The room goes black for a few seconds,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.510
then the lights come back on.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.900
Contrast this with the nearly continuous

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.793
blinding light from episode one.

00:03:15.038 --> 00:03:16.903
So the next question:

00:03:16.903 --> 00:03:18.733
How would the Soviets have gotten Hopper

00:03:18.733 --> 00:03:20.710
out of the Upside Down?

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.560
We know they've been studying the gate,

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.790
as we saw in episode one.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.870
We also see in the mid-credits scene

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.390
that they have access to
beings from the Upside Down,

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.325
i.e., a demogorgon.

00:03:31.325 --> 00:03:33.573
They could've also gone
back into the Upside Down

00:03:33.573 --> 00:03:35.010
and taken Hopper out.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.470
Hopper was in the Upside
Down a few times before,

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:41.060
so he may have been able to survive there.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.100
In season two, Hopper and Dustin

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.780
inhaled spores in the Upside Down tunnel.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.500
Dustin had his face
covered, but Hopper didn't,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.886
and the side effects of this
has never really been shown

00:03:50.886 --> 00:03:52.145
since that episode.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.310
Would whatever was in these spores

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.483
help him survive in the Upside Down?

00:03:58.977 --> 00:04:00.910
After the blast, we see characters

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.730
reacting to news of Billy
and Hopper's deaths.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.730
There's a WCPK-TV news
segment that pops up.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.730
A sign that previously
read "Welcome to Hawkins"

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.160
now reads "Welcome to Hell,"

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.947
echoing Hopper's final
line of season three,

00:04:16.947 --> 00:04:18.910
"See you in hell".

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.250
Teasing a character death
at the end of the season

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.600
isn't new to "Stranger Things."

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.960
In season one, it was
suggested Eleven had died,

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.860
only for her to return.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.990
The same song that plays
at the end of this season,

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.280
a version of "Heroes" by David Bowie,

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.246
also played when we thought Will had died.

00:04:42.807 --> 00:04:45.090
Also, "Stranger Things" has shied away

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.690
from a central-character death
for nearly three seasons.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.897
While supporting-cast members
like Bob, Barb, and now Billy

00:04:52.897 --> 00:04:55.060
are potentially more disposable,

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.403
it's difficult to imagine
season four without Hopper.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.890
The audience is teased with a
will-they-won't-they storyline

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:05.480
with Joyce and Hopper
throughout this season,

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.164
cumulating in their emotional
glance at one another

00:05:08.164 --> 00:05:09.890
through the partition.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:12.299
There seems to be little
point in going so far

00:05:12.299 --> 00:05:13.907
with this budding romance

00:05:13.907 --> 00:05:16.560
if there won't be a true resolution.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.440
Joyce has already seen Bob, her
previous love interest, die,

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:23.653
so it would seem like lazy
writing to repeat this arc.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.300
While Eleven is packing,
she reads a letter.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.330
It's Hopper's speech to her and Mike

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.800
that he wanted to say to them
but never did in episode one.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.890
This speech encourages Eleven's growth

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.117
via the pain that she's suffered:

00:05:38.117 --> 00:05:42.370
"When life hurts you, because
it will, remember the hurt."

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:43.740
It also teases that Hopper

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.037
is just on the other side of a door:

00:05:46.037 --> 00:05:47.977
"For the sake of your poor old Dad,

00:05:47.977 --> 00:05:50.650
keep the door open three inches."

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.030
Will Eleven and her friends

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.753
have to reopen a door to get Hopper back?

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.740
What did you think about the ending?

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.027
Comment below.

